# How to cite LITMO. Machine-readable description of the software: codemeta.json.
# Still to add: the Zenodo DOI (`doi:` and an `identifiers` entry) and, once accepted, the paper as
# `preferred-citation`.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use LITMO, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "LITMO: LITerature-driven Modular Ontologies"
abstract: >-
  LITMO is an open-source web tool that builds modular OWL ontologies from a field's literature with a large
  language model as a drafting partner. It follows the Modular Ontology Modeling (MOMo) methodology: use case,
  competency questions, key notions, design decisions and modules come first, the OWL file last, and the team
  decides at every step. Papers are read one by one into a concept inventory with verified quotes; each generated
  class records its sources; competency questions and hold-out questions from the literature run as SPARQL tests,
  and a reasoner checks the result.
authors:
  - family-names: Moosmann
    given-names: Paul
    email: paul.moosmann@fit.fraunhofer.de
    affiliation: "Fraunhofer Institute for Applied Information Technology FIT"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Fraunhofer-FIT-DSAI/LITMO"
url: "https://github.com/Fraunhofer-FIT-DSAI/LITMO"
license: Apache-2.0
version: "0.3"
date-released: "2026-11-24"
keywords:
  - ontology engineering
  - large language models
  - modular ontologies
  - Modular Ontology Modeling
  - competency questions
  - OWL
  - literature
